course: medicine
lesson: 30
title: Germs, the immune system and hand-washing
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Germs are tiny living things, bacteria, viruses and fungi, far too small to see without a microscope; most bacteria are harmless or even helpful, and only some make us ill.
explain: Your immune system is like an army inside you: white blood cells hunt germs, some make antibodies that stick to them, and memory cells remember each enemy for next time.
explain: When you graze your knee and it goes red, warm and puffy, that is the army arriving: blood brings white cells to the spot, and the swelling is part of the fight.
explain: Two hundred years ago doctors thought illness came from bad air; then Semmelweis made doctors wash their hands and fewer mothers died, and Pasteur and Koch proved that germs cause disease.
explain: Most germs reach your mouth on your own hands, which is why twenty seconds with soap, about two Happy Birthdays, is one of the most powerful medicines there is.
fact: Germs include bacteria, viruses and fungi; most bacteria are harmless or helpful, and your gut holds trillions of useful ones.
fact: Bacteria are single cells too small to see without a microscope, and viruses are smaller still.
fact: White blood cells find and destroy germs, and some make antibodies that stick to germs and are remembered for next time.
fact: No single food or drink can boost the immune system; sleep, a good diet and exercise keep it working well.
fact: Washing hands with soap and water for about 20 seconds, including thumbs, between fingers and under nails, removes germs that cause colds and tummy bugs.
fact: Ordinary soap works well, and antibacterial soap has not been shown to be better for everyday use.
fact: A sneeze can spray droplets several metres, so the advice is to catch it in a tissue, bin it, then wash your hands, or sneeze into your elbow.
fact: The 5-second rule is a myth: bacteria can move onto dropped food in under a second, depending on the food and the floor.
fact: Lymph nodes, often called glands, are part of the immune system and swell while fighting an infection, such as in the neck during a cold.
fact: Inflammation, redness, warmth and swelling, is the body sending blood and white cells to fight germs, but spreading redness with fever needs a doctor.
fact: In the 1840s Ignaz Semmelweis showed that doctors washing their hands saved mothers' lives, and in the 1860s to 1880s Louis Pasteur and Robert Koch proved that germs cause disease.
fact: Before germ theory, many people believed illness came from bad air, called miasma.
fact: Contact with nature and many kinds of microbes may help a child's immune system develop, though scientists are still studying the details.
exercise: Which of these are germs? | Bacteria, viruses and fungi | Vitamins and minerals in food | Red blood cells | Antibodies made by the body
exercise: Are all bacteria harmful? | No, most are harmless or helpful | Yes, every single one of them | Only the ones that live in the gut | Only the ones about in summer
exercise: Can you see bacteria with your eyes alone? | No, you need a microscope | Yes, as tiny grey dots if you look closely | Yes, as the dirt you see on hands | Only in the dark, when they glow
exercise: Which are smaller, bacteria or viruses? | Viruses | Bacteria, by a long way | Both are exactly the same size | It changes every day
exercise: What do white blood cells do? | Find and destroy germs | Carry oxygen round the body | Make your bones grow longer | Digest food in the stomach
exercise: What are antibodies? | Proteins that stick to germs and are remembered | Good germs that fight the bad germs for you | Tiny white blood cells | A kind of vitamin found in fruit
exercise: How long should you wash your hands with soap? | About 20 seconds | About 2 seconds | About 5 minutes, scrubbing hard | Just a quick rinse under the tap
exercise: Is antibacterial soap better than normal soap for everyday use? | It hasn't been shown to be better | Yes, much better, it kills everything | Yes, but it only kills the viruses | Normal soap does nothing to germs
exercise: What's the advice for a sneeze? | Catch it in a tissue or your elbow, bin it, wash hands | Sneeze into your hands and wipe them on your jumper | Sneeze towards the floor so germs drop down | Hold it in always
exercise: Is the 5-second rule true? | No, bacteria can move onto food in under a second | Yes, germs need five whole seconds to climb on | Yes, but only on a clean carpet | It's 10 seconds really, not five
exercise: Why can a gland in your neck swell during a cold? | Lymph nodes swell while fighting infection | The cold air freezes the neck and it puffs up | Snot drains down and collects there | You've eaten too much
exercise: Why did your scratch go red and warm? | Blood brought white cells to fight germs | The germs are painting it red from inside | The skin is slowly burning up | The plaster was stuck on far too tight
exercise: Who proved that germs cause disease? | Louis Pasteur and Robert Koch | Edward Jenner and Alexander Fleming | Isaac Newton | Charles Darwin and his grandfather
exercise: Who made doctors wash their hands in the 1840s? | Ignaz Semmelweis | Florence Nightingale | Alexander Fleming | Louis Braille
exercise: What did people think caused illness before germ theory? | Bad air | Too much sunshine | Eating green vegetables | Reading books in bed
exercise: Can one food or drink boost your immune system? | No, sleep, good food and exercise keep it working | Yes, a big glass of orange juice every day | Yes, a special yoghurt with good germs in | Yes, energy drinks
test: Dropped my chips, picked em up in 2 secs, all good yeah? | No, bacteria can get on in under a second | Yes, it's the 5-second rule, you're well safe | Yes, chips are germ-proof | Only if you blow on them first
test: Dad says "mud's good for you". What's fair? | Outdoor play and microbes may help the immune system, then wash before eating | Mud cures colds | Mud is always dangerous and should never be touched by children | Eat a bit of mud daily
test: What would happen if doctors stopped washing their hands between patients? | Germs would spread from patient to patient | Nothing, germs don't live on clean-looking hands | Patients would get better faster | Only the doctors themselves would get ill
test: Ben coughs into his hand then opens the door. What's the problem? | Germs from his hand go onto the handle for others | Nothing, coughs don't carry any germs at all | The door handle gets sticky, that's all | Germs die on hands the moment they land there
test: "u got a lump in ur neck, that's cancer" — after a cold. Most likely? | A swollen lymph node fighting the cold | Definitely cancer, see a surgeon today | A broken bone | A bee sting
test: A cut gets red, hot, spreads, and you get a fever. What then? | See a doctor, the infection may be spreading | Ignore it, red always means it is healing well | Squeeze it hard to push the germs out | Put mud on it
test: Mum buys super antibacterial soap "to kill 99.9%". Is ordinary soap enough? | Yes, ordinary soap and good washing work well | No, normal soap does nothing at all against germs | Only antibacterial soap works properly | Only very hot water works
test: Cartoon shows germs as huge green monsters on a sandwich. What's real? | Germs are far too small to see without a microscope | Germs really are that big, they just hide | Germs are only ever green | Sandwiches have no germs on them
test: Why do you wash between fingers and under nails, not just palms? | Germs hide there too | Palms have no skin for germs to sit on | Nails clean themselves | It looks nicer
test: In the 1800s a doctor says illness comes from bad smells. What showed he was wrong? | Experiments proved tiny germs cause infections | Nothing, he was right about the bad smells | Perfume was found to cure most diseases | Smells were banned
test: Your gut has trillions of bacteria. Are you ill? | No, most are helpful and help digestion | Yes, very ill, and you should be in bed | Yes, you need antibiotics to clear them out | Only if you can feel them
exam: Identify the principal function of white blood cells within the human body. | The detection and destruction of pathogens | The transport of oxygen to the tissues of the body | The formation of bone | The digestion of food within the stomach
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the so-called five-second rule. | Bacteria may be transferred to food in less than one second | Bacteria require a minimum of five seconds to transfer to food | Bacteria cannot transfer to dry food | The rule is endorsed by food safety authorities
exam: Ascertain the scientists credited with demonstrating, in the nineteenth century, that micro-organisms cause infectious disease. | Louis Pasteur and Robert Koch | Edward Jenner and Joseph Priestley | Isaac Newton and Robert Hooke | Alexander Fleming and Howard Florey
exam: Determine the duration of hand-washing with soap and water ordinarily recommended. | Approximately twenty seconds | Approximately two seconds | Approximately five minutes | No minimum duration is recommended
